WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.890
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:06.000
The corner of the universe that we live in
is pretty quiet; there’s nothing nearby

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.720
likely to explode, swallow us or just generally
try to ruin our weekend.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.320
But that’s not to say that there aren’t
some terrifying things out there in the darkness

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.119
that would make you cower under your bed with
Mr Fluffles and hope the solar system is just

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:23.900
how you left it when you finally crawl out
again.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.470
Today, we’re looking at some of the most
terrifying monsters we have discovered in

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.890
the universe.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:40.420
Brontophobia is the fear of lightning and
there are estimated to be 24,000 lightning-related

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.710
deaths a year.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:47.670
A typical lightning strike is about 30,000
amps and even these can vaporise metal and

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.730
fuse sand into glass.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:54.629
But a team at the University of Toronto have
found the highest electrical current that

00:00:54.629 --> 00:01:02.350
has ever been measured, somewhere out in space,
measuring an enormous 1018 amps, which is

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.719
the same as a trillion of our lightning bolts
here on Earth.

00:01:06.719 --> 00:01:09.710
So what’s causing this massive surge of
electricity?

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:12.439
Has Thor been on the steroids again?

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:19.149
It’s actually down to a gigantic black hole
at the core of a galaxy called 3C303.

00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:24.529
The magnetic fields that it produces are moving
and interacting and this is what generates

00:01:24.529 --> 00:01:28.329
the massive electrical surge, like a supersized
dynamo.

00:01:28.329 --> 00:01:33.849
And if we to manage to avoid being struck
by lightning, that doesn’t mean were safe

00:01:33.849 --> 00:01:37.380
from all burning up like a Walmart on Black
Friday.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.219
In every galaxy there are stars moving so
quickly that they are able to escape the pull

00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:45.490
of gravity and race through space unchecked.

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:51.419
Stars, much like those of Hollywood, are single
much less than you think.

00:01:51.419 --> 00:01:56.740
About half the stars in our galaxy form a
binary pair, where two stars orbit around

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.249
each other in a beautiful cosmic dance.

00:01:59.249 --> 00:02:05.009
Our sun has to sit on its own, in the corner
like the ugly fat kid at a dance.

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.179
So sometimes, when these binary pairs are
dragged towards the large black hole that

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:14.269
always sits at the centre of every galaxy,
one of the stars can be ripped free and hurled

00:02:14.269 --> 00:02:17.340
off at a speed of 10,000 kilometres per second.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:23.480
The other is swallowed into the black hole,
like when that lonely fat kid finds the buffet

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.480
table.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.159
And if such a rogue star came anywhere near
our solar system, there’s not very much

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:33.849
we could do to stop it as it burned us up
and its enormous gravity pulled everything

00:02:33.849 --> 00:02:35.079
out of orbit.

00:02:35.079 --> 00:02:39.810
The likelihood of this is fortunately very
low though, so if you do see a bright dot

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:44.750
in the sky, don’t stop doing your homework
and wait for the world to end just yet, it’s

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.549
probably just an aeroplane or a really shiny
pigeon.

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:55.569
At some point our galaxy is going to collide
with the Andromeda galaxy so make sure that

00:02:55.569 --> 00:03:00.260
you don’t put anything in the diary for
about 3 billion years in the future, you know;

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.260
just in case.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.129
When the two combine, the black holes at their
centres will either merge into a supermassive

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:11.840
black hole, or they form a binary pair, just
like the stars we mentioned earlier.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:17.170
The compressed matter around these supermassive
black holes becomes a quasar, which stands

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:23.079
for quasi-stellar radio source, since they
emit similar electromagnetic radiation to

00:03:23.079 --> 00:03:26.400
stars, including radio-waves and visible light.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:32.620
They are incredibly bright; you’d need 100
Milky Ways just to match them.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:38.739
Just one quasar is enough to use up our entire
stock pile of sunglasses, but the Huge Large

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:45.680
Quasar Group, who names this crap, is a string
of 73 quasars that spreads 4 billion light

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.730
years across, making it the second largest
structure in the known universe.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:56.430
The biggest of which is the fancily named
Hercules-Corona Borealis Great Wall, okay

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:02.560
that’s slightly better, a group of 14 gamma
ray burst walls, which are very bright objects

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.469
caused by dying stars.

00:04:04.469 --> 00:04:09.529
Both of these gigantic monsters you will want
to avoid if you value your eyesight.

00:04:09.529 --> 00:04:14.889
Sometimes, when you have a massive star, it
goes supernova and collapses under its own

00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:20.620
weight and ends up as a neutron star, which
are hyper dense balls about 11 kilometres

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.380
across yet they can have the mass of double
our sun.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:26.840
How is this possible?

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.370
How can you fit that much into such a ridiculously
small space?

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.390
Are neutron stars made by IKEA?

00:04:32.390 --> 00:04:37.800
No, there is a scientific explanation; as
anyone who has ever tried to pack a week’s

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.020
clothes into their hand luggage will tell
you, if you push things together that tightly,

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:44.639
they will push back.

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:49.590
Those same people will tell you it wasn’t
worth the £11 they saved on luggage fees

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.319
when their underwear and spare teabags burst
all over security control.

00:04:53.319 --> 00:04:58.470
The neutron stars are, unsurprisingly, made
up of neutrons.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.120
When you pack them together that tightly,
the only thing that stops it collapsing into

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.699
a black hole is this repulsion between the
particles, which only becomes powerful at

00:05:07.699 --> 00:05:09.699
such immense densities.

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:16.259
A neutron star is so dense that just a matchbox
sized amount would weigh about 13 million

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:18.770
tonnes on Earth.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:26.110
Occasionally these neutron stars become magnetars,
where they produce such a powerful magnetic

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:30.289
field that they even alter the vacuum of space
around them.

00:05:30.289 --> 00:05:36.240
They are the most powerful magnets in the
universe, so if you visit, remember to leave

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.160
behind your credit card and take out your
nipple piercing.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.160
Ouch.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:47.259
Stars are messy little things; not only do
they pump out a wide spectrum of electromagnetic

00:05:47.259 --> 00:05:53.050
radiation, but when they are finished, they
often leave behind huge clouds of dust, called

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:54.229
a nebula.

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:58.410
If you were up close, you probably wouldn’t
notice since it’s spread out more thinly

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:01.620
than any artificial vacuum we’ve ever made
on earth.

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.070
But from far enough away, we can spot what
remains of the star, and if we watch for long

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:14.250
enough, we’ll probably see surrounding solar
systems complaining about their messy neighbour.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:19.160
These nebulae can be hundreds of light years
across and while our clouds on earth are often

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:25.379
fat, cute and fluffy, these space giants form
strange and twisted shapes that truly look

00:06:25.379 --> 00:06:28.110
like monsters of the universe.

00:06:28.110 --> 00:06:32.879
Just seven thousand light years away, within
the Eagle Nebula, there are the towering space

00:06:32.879 --> 00:06:36.000
worms known as The Pillars of Creation.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:40.910
These dark clouds will slowly draw together
and form the stars of the future, where their

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:46.530
simple hydrogen will heat and eventually produce
all the rich and complicated material like

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.180
we see here on earth.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:51.520
How about a real monster this time?

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.930
Just when you thought that space was quiet
as the grave, you hear a horrifying moan;

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:57.470
it’s a Zombie Star.

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:02.349
I’m not sure you can defeat this one with
a cricket bat to the head, so probably best

00:07:02.349 --> 00:07:06.509
to head down the Winchester, bar the doors,
and hope it doesn’t find you.

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:11.930
This is the result of a binary pair of stars,
where one of them is a white dwarf.

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:16.040
A white dwarf is similar to a neutron star
except that the original star didn’t have

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:20.830
enough mass to fully collapse into a neutron
star, so you’re left with a ball of mostly

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:22.170
carbon and oxygen.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:27.730
There’s no fusion reaction going on anymore,
so it’s just a corpse left out on the porch,

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.389
slowly losing its heat.

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:34.729
But when you have a binary pair, it’s possible
that the white dwarf will leech energy off

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:40.159
its partner and slowly regain the mass it
needs to come alive again, kick starting the

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:45.360
fusion reaction, probably resulting in a final
rampaging supernova.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.729
Such stars are fittingly known as “zombie
stars”.

00:07:48.729 --> 00:07:54.800
It’s a similar story for vampire stars,
also known as blue stragglers.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.979
We discovered stars that seemed to be much
hotter than those in their local area, and

00:07:58.979 --> 00:08:02.569
when it comes to stars, hotter means younger.

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:06.639
But since they all formed from the same nebula;
how can this happen?

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:13.160
Well, like with the zombie, we think one star
sucks the life out of the other, giving it

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.710
that youthful glow we all long for.

00:08:15.710 --> 00:08:20.960
So put down your quinoa cream, pour away that
disgusting kale juice, and stop going to bloody

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.150
Zumba class, all you need to do is eat a star.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.350
Lastly, a weird one.

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:32.080
If you were an interstellar explorer, living
off vacuum dried protein bars and drinking

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.390
your own recycled liquids, there are probably
times you stare out into the frozen blackness

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:40.770
and think; god dammit I need a drink.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.910
Well ask and ye shall receive.

00:08:42.910 --> 00:08:48.230
Sagittarius B2 is a 150 light year long booze
stop.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:54.850
This dust cloud within the Milky Way contains
vast, vast quantities of ethyl formate, which

00:08:54.850 --> 00:08:58.420
give it the unique flavour of raspberries
and rum.

00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:04.840
There’s also enough ethyl alcohol there
to get everyone on earth drunk for trillions

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:05.840
of years.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:11.210
So, if NASA are really worried about the public
losing interest in the space program, just

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:16.480
tell them for a few billion dollars, we’re
going to build a ship and go and raid god’s

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:17.680
liquor cabinet.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:22.340
Maybe this is why we’ve yet to meet alien
life, they’re all waiting for us to join

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:26.270
them at the gigantic bar in the sky.

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:31.200
With all these monsters creeping around, perhaps
we should be grateful that all we have to

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:37.410
deal with is one sun, the occasional asteroid
and all those other weird monsters, I mean

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:42.080
people, that we’ve managed to get stuck
on this little rock with; I think we can all

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.360
raise a rum and raspberry to that, right?

00:09:44.360 --> 00:10:09.850
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

